      en: "The Citadel of Damascus is a medieval fortress located in the northwest corner of the old city. It was originally built by the Seljuks in the 11th century and later expanded by the Ayyubids, especially Sultan al-Adil I in the early 13th century. The citadel has an irregular plan following the terrain, with walls stretching about 2.5 km. It served as the residence of rulers and a military garrison. It withstood sieges by the Mongols (1260) and Timur (1401). The citadel now houses the National Museum of Military History, showcasing armor, weapons, and artifacts from the Islamic period.",
